{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T07:56:17.428Z","ordinal":13976,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_088039b628d127e6016a9684e3c0e887d19b287c61133286c4","status":"completed","call_id":"call_pouRqHC8urie7390Mvh9HSoF","name":"exec","input":"const patch = \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Add File: /lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/CD3_OPTIMIZATION_AND_P4FLAT_COMPARISON.md\\n+# CD3 optimization and P4Flat comparison\\n+\\n+Date: 2026-09-01 UTC\\n+\\n+## 1. Executive conclusion\\n+\\n+The final CD3 implementation is not uniformly faster than P4Flat in every\\n+isolated operation. Its advantage comes from combining a denser lossless\\n+format with substantially faster CPU encoding, less LPDDR-to-HBM traffic, and\\n+a Hopper WGMMA fused path tuned for the decode batch sizes used by the serving\\n+workload.\\n+\\n+On the tested GH200 and Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct configuration:\\n+\\n+- Total compressed weight size is about 3.1% smaller: CD3 ratio is about\\n+  1.368x versus P4Flat 1.326x.\\n+- The LPDDR-resident portion transferred per layer in frozen-split mode is\\n+  376.586 MiB for CD3 versus 414.502 MiB for P4Flat, 9.15% fewer bytes.\\n+- CPU weight encoding of one layer's four fused projections is 14.9x to 28.1x\\n+  faster across the recorded runs. The latest median-of-five run was\\n+  128.830 ms versus 3620.297 ms.\\n+- Steady-state CPU KV encoding is 1.20x to 2.88x faster over N=8..2048.\\n+- Weight standalone H2D+decode is 4.36% faster overall. The H2D part is 10.89%\\n+  faster, while isolated resident decode is 1.45% slower.\\n+- In the latency-critical fused route, the sum of the four layer-40 projection\\n+  medians is 7.08% faster than P4Flat at N=64 and 14.62% faster at N=128 when\\n+  each codec uses its production route.\\n+- KV H2D-only and resident-decode-only are consistently faster in CD3, but the\\n+  complete production `decompress_batch` call is dispatch-dominated at small\\n+  N. Repeated fresh-process results range from 6--12% slower at N=8/64/256 to\\n+  statistical parity at N=1024. It is not valid to claim a robust KV\\n+  H2D+decode win at small N from the current measurements.\\n+\\n+There is not yet a clean final-revision, apples-to-apples full serving\\n+TTFT/TPOT run of final CD3 against final P4Flat. The percentages above are\\n+component and per-layer route measurements. Older 2026-08-27 serving results\\n+predate the final CD3 kernels and routing, so they are intentionally excluded\\n+from the headline comparison.\\n+\\n+## 2. Test platform and terminology\\n+\\n+- GPU/system: NVIDIA GH200, compute capability 9.0, Grace ARM CPU, CUDA 13.2.\\n+- Model weights: real `meta-llama/Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct`, TP=1, BF16.\\n+- Representative layer: transformer layer 40 unless otherwise stated.\\n+- Fused serving weights:\\n+  - QKV: 10240 x 8192, 160 MiB raw BF16.\\n+  - O: 8192 x 8192, 128 MiB.\\n+  - GateUp: 57344 x 8192, 896 MiB.\\n+  - Down: 8192 x 28672, 448 MiB.\\n+  - Total per layer: 1632 MiB (1.59375 GiB).\\n+- `N` is the flattened activation row/token count presented to a linear layer.\\n+- All retained codec paths are lossless; reported comparisons passed raw BF16\\n+  bit-exact checks.\\n+\\n+The measurements use the following distinct definitions:\\n+\\n+- CPU encode: BF16 input is on the CPU. Time includes production codec CPU\\n+  work and synchronization, but never a CUDA encode kernel.\\n+- Resident decode: compressed data is already in GPU HBM. Only the GPU decode\\n+  kernel is timed.\\n+- H2D-only: only the LPDDR/pinned-host buffers required for the selected path\\n+  are copied into preallocated HBM staging. On GH200 this is Grace LPDDR to GPU\\n+  HBM over NVLink-C2C, not a conventional discrete-GPU PCIe transfer.\\n+- H2D+decode: the production transfer and decode operation is timed. It is an\\n+  independent measurement, not necessarily the arithmetic sum of separately\\n+  measured H2D and decode medians.\\n+- Fused: compressed weight reconstruction occurs in the register-A WGMMA\\n+  kernel and no dense BF16 weight is emitted to HBM.\\n+- Standalone: the full BF16 weight is reconstructed in HBM and then consumed\\n+  by a separate GEMM.\\n+- Raw: the full uncompressed BF16 weight is transferred and no codec decode\\n+  kernel runs.\\n+\\n+## 3. Format and memory footprint\\n+\\n+CD3 preserves the BF16 sign and mantissa byte verbatim and encodes the exponent\\n+as a 3-bit delta from one base exponent per 16 values. A 64x64 tile therefore\\n+contains 1536 code bytes, 256 base bytes, and a sparse exception stream.\\n+Exceptions use a 20-bit `(12-bit tile position, 8-bit exponent)` entry, packed\\n+three entries into each 64-bit word (`Packed20x3`).\\n+\\n+Codes 0 through 7 are all normal CD3 deltas. An exception temporarily carries\\n+code 0 and is distinguished by its Packed20 position/mask. Consequently code 7\\n+cannot be used as an exception sentinel without changing the format. The\\n+Escape7 experiment increased exceptions by 85.6%, slowed decode by 11.11%, and\\n+would reduce the expected KV compression ratio to about 1.3347x, close to\\n+P4Flat's 1.3259x.\\n+\\n+For representative layer-40 weights:\\n+\\n+| Metric | CD3 | P4Flat | CD3 change |\\n+|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n+| Total compression ratio | 1.368x | 1.326x | 3.1% fewer compressed bytes |\\n+| Frozen-split LPDDR transfer/layer | 376.586 MiB | 414.502 MiB | 9.15% fewer bytes |\\n+\\n+Both codecs retain sign/mantissa in HBM for frozen-split weights. CD3 also\\n+retains tile exception offsets in HBM. The table includes all transferred\\n+metadata, so auxiliary structures are accounted for in the comparison.\\n+\\n+KV is different: offloaded KV does not retain the sign/mantissa half in HBM.\\n+The selected K and V compressed slot blobs are transferred in full and decoded\\n+directly into the final GPU KV cache.\\n+\\n+## 4. CPU encoding\\n+\\n+### 4.1 Weight CPU encode\\n+\\n+Input was the four real layer-40 fused BF16 CPU tensors listed above. Each\\n+codec ran in a fresh process on NUMA node 0, with one untimed warm-up and five\\n+timed repetitions per projection; the table contains medians. No GPU encoder\\n+was called.\\n+\\n+| Projection | CD3 (ms) | P4Flat (ms) | Speedup |\\n+|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n+| QKV | 15.824 | 354.944 | 22.4x |\\n+| O | 12.710 | 285.589 | 22.5x |\\n+| GateUp | 79.161 | 1987.135 | 25.1x |\\n+| Down | 21.135 | 992.629 | 47.0x |\\n+| Sum of medians | 128.830 | 3620.297 | 28.1x |\\n+\\n+An earlier recorded run was 130.7 ms versus 1951.9 ms (14.9x). CD3 is stable\\n+around 129--131 ms, while P4Flat CPU time is sensitive to host/OpenMP state.\\n+The defensible conclusion is an order-of-magnitude win, observed between\\n+14.9x and 28.1x, rather than one universal multiplier.\\n+\\n+Weight encode is normally an offline/load-time step, so this gain mostly\\n+reduces initialization/preprocessing time and prevents codec preparation from\\n+stealing GPU SM cycles; it is not added to token latency.\\n+\\n+### 4.2 KV CPU encode\\n+\\n+Input was real cached K and V blocks from\\n+`/root/projects/ats-benchmarks/benchmarks/compress/kv_blocks_cache.pt`, each\\n+block shaped `[16,8,128]` BF16. K+V is 64 KiB per block. The production\\n+`compress_batch_async_from_host` plus `wait_compress_async` path was measured\\n+with one compression worker, `OMP=all`, NUMA node 0, one warm-up and 20 timed\\n+iterations. GPU decode was used only after timing to verify bit exactness.\\n+\\n+| Blocks N | K+V input | CD3 median | P4Flat median | CD3 speedup |\\n+|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n+| 8 | 0.5 MiB | 0.17 ms | 0.49 ms | 2.88x |\\n+| 64 | 4 MiB | 0.28 ms | 0.55 ms | 1.96x |\\n+| 128 | 8 MiB | 0.40 ms | 0.60 ms | 1.50x |\\n+| 256 | 16 MiB | 0.49 ms | 0.68 ms | 1.39x |\\n+| 1024 | 64 MiB | 1.04 ms | 1.25 ms | 1.20x |\\n+| 2048 | 128 MiB | 1.99 ms | 2.48 ms | 1.25x |\\n+\\n+## 5. Weight standalone H2D/decode comparison\\n+\\n+This used `benchmarks/weight_cpu_h2d_decode.py` with the real layer-40 fused\\n+weights, CPU encoding for both codecs, 10 warm-ups and 50 samples. Encode and\\n+setup were outside CUDA timing. Sign/mantissa stayed in HBM; only the\\n+LPDDR-side exponent representation was transferred using the same\\n+`cudaMemcpyBatchAsync` helper for both codecs.\\n+\\n+| Sum across QKV/O/GateUp/Down | CD3 | P4Flat | CD3 change |\\n+|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n+| H2D-only | 1.2027 ms | 1.3497 ms | 10.89% faster |\\n+| Resident decode-only | 0.9211 ms | 0.9079 ms | 1.45% slower |\\n+| Serialized H2D+decode | 2.1286 ms | 2.2256 ms | 4.36% faster |\\n+\\n+Thus the weight win is a transfer win, not a pure decoder win. CD3's 3-bit\\n+codes reduce the LPDDR traffic enough to compensate for a slightly more\\n+expensive decoder. Per-projection H2D+decode medians were also all lower for\\n+CD3: QKV 0.2183 vs 0.2338 ms, O 0.1808 vs 0.1904 ms, GateUp 1.1430 vs\\n+1.1920 ms, and Down 0.5864 vs 0.6095 ms.\\n+\\n+## 6. KV H2D/decode comparison\\n+\\n+The input was the same real K/V block cache as the CPU encode test. Each codec\\n+and block count ran in a new process with CPU encoding, one worker, 20 warm-ups\\n+and 100 CUDA-event samples. Three complete rounds were run; the table reports\\n+the median of the three per-process medians. No GPU encoder was called.\\n+\\n+| N | CD3 H2D | P4 H2D | CD3 resident decode | P4 resident decode | CD3 production total | P4 production total |\\n+|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n+| 8 | 0.0171 | 0.0289 | 0.0309 | 0.0458 | 0.0703 | 0.0629 |\\n+| 64 | 0.0260 | 0.0376 | 0.0287 | 0.0448 | 0.0686 | 0.0647 |\\n+| 256 | 0.0559 | 0.0666 | 0.0371 | 0.0466 | 0.1004 | 0.0915 |\\n+| 1024 | 0.1730 | 0.1849 | 0.0668 | 0.0736 | 0.2486 | 0.2497 |\\n+\\n+All values are milliseconds. CD3 reduces H2D-only by 40.8%, 30.9%, 16.1%,\\n+and 6.4%, and resident decode by 32.5%, 35.9%, 20.4%, and 9.2% at the four\\n+sizes. However, the complete production call is 11.8%, 6.0%, and 9.7% slower\\n+at N=8/64/256 and only 0.4% faster at N=1024. At these sub-0.1-ms sizes,\\n+Python/native dispatch, plan lookup, stream/event handling, and K/V submission\\n+overhead dominate the kernels that were optimized.\\n+\\n+An earlier single measurement recorded a more favorable CD3 production total\\n+(0.065/0.065/0.102/0.251 ms versus P4Flat 0.063/0.070/0.107/0.302 ms). The\\n+fresh three-round result above supersedes that single run for conclusions: the\\n+separate H2D and decode improvements are robust, while small-N production\\n+total ordering is not.\\n+\\n+## 7. Fused decode+GEMM comparison\\n+\\n+Input weights were the four real layer-40 projections. Activations were seeded\\n+random BF16 `[N,K]` tensors. Sign/mantissa stayed resident in HBM and the\\n+LPDDR-side metadata was copied before the compute stream ran. L2 was flushed\\n+for every sample; results are P50 after 10 warm-ups and 50 iterations. The\\n+numbers below sum the four independently measured projection medians, so they\\n+are a per-layer component estimate, not an end-to-end TPOT result.\\n+\\n+Codec encode was setup only and excluded from timing. CD3 setup used its\\n+default CPU weight encoder; the P4Flat benchmark helper prepared its object\\n+with the existing GPU encoder. This does not affect the timed H2D/kernel\\n+comparison because both produce the production bit-exact format, but it must\\n+not be read as an encode comparison.\\n+\\n+| N | P4Flat production route | CD3 production route | P4 total | CD3 total | CD3 gain |\\n+|---:|:---|:---|---:|---:|---:|\\n+| 32 | all fused | all fused | 2.2400 ms | 2.0096 ms | 10.28% |\\n+| 48 | all fused | all fused | 2.2539 ms | 2.0574 ms | 8.72% |\\n+| 64 | all fused | all fused | 2.2644 ms | 2.1041 ms | 7.08% |\\n+| 128 | QKV standalone, other three fused | all fused | 2.6661 ms | 2.2762 ms | 14.62% |\\n+\\n+At N=128, forcing P4Flat QKV to remain fused gives 2.8080 ms versus CD3\\n+2.2756 ms, a 18.96% total reduction. The forced fused-kernel-only sums are\\n+1.5953 ms versus 1.1727 ms, a 26.49% reduction. This forced result is useful\\n+for comparing kernels, but the 14.62% production-policy result is the fair\\n+serving route comparison.\\n+\\n+Relative to the early CD3 fused baseline discussed during development:\\n+\\n+- At N=64, CD3 kernel sum moved from 1.322 ms to 0.996 ms, about 24.7% lower.\\n+  It changed from 22.9% slower than P4Flat (1.076 ms) to 5.1% faster than the\\n+  current P4Flat kernel sum (1.050 ms).\\n+- At N=128, CD3 moved from 1.573 ms to 1.173 ms, about 25.4% lower. Its\\n+  forced-fused lead over P4Flat grew from about 4.6% to 26.5%.\\n+\\n+## 8. Production route policy\\n+\\n+The final CD3 policy for the tested model/configuration is:\\n+\\n+- N <= 128: all QKV/O/GateUp/Down use fused decode+WGMMA.\\n+- Eager prefill N=129..1855: transfer compressed metadata, standalone-decode\\n+  the weight, then run GEMM.\\n+- Eager prefill N >= 1856: transfer raw BF16 and skip codec decode.\\n+\\n+Current P4Flat uses QKV fused only through N=64 and O/GateUp/Down through\\n+N=128. Its frozen-split standalone/raw prefill cutoff is 1330.\\n+\\n+The CD3 standalone/raw cutoff was measured end-to-end with Llama-70B, not\\n+derived only from a bandwidth model. Five runs were made at each point and the\\n+first-use sample was discarded. At N=1824, standalone was 470.965 ms versus\\n+489.595 ms raw; at N=1856, raw was 459.620 ms versus 480.025 ms standalone.\\n+Raw remained ahead through N=3072. An unforced run confirmed the route switch\\n+exactly at 1856.\\n+\\n+The 1856 versus 1330 cutoff should not be presented as a 39.5% direct\\n+performance speedup: it says that CD3 remains worth decoding over a 526-token\\n+wider range under this residency/fabric/model combination.\\n+\\n+An all-80-layer CD3 QKV sweep found fused faster on 79/80 layers and saved\\n+0.508/0.595/1.211 ms in aggregate at N=80/96/128 versus QKV standalone. Layer\\n+0 was the exception-heavy outlier. At full engine level, all-CD3-fused versus\\n+CD3-QKV-standalone TPOT P50 was 171.90/188.40/219.26 ms versus\\n+172.20/187.96/219.30 ms for batch 80/96/128: effectively tied because other\\n+serving work masks the per-layer difference. This is a CD3 route ablation, not\\n+a CD3-versus-P4Flat end-to-end result.\\n+\\n+## 9. Retained optimization work\\n+\\n+### 9.1 CPU encoder\\n+\\n+- Block-owner one-pass encoding: one OpenMP owner completes a KV block in\\n+  tile/thread/item order, eliminating global prefix buffers, barriers, and the\\n+  second source-exponent read.\\n+- Min/max exception-free fast path: if 16 exponents fit in one 3-bit range,\\n+  candidate search and per-element exception work are skipped.\\n+- Direct local Packed20x3 construction with uninitialized temporary exception\\n+  scratch; only valid entries are compacted.\\n+- NEON extraction of exponent/sign-mantissa and hierarchical packing of 16\\n+  three-bit codes.\\n+- SVE2 predicate counting only on the uncommon exception path.\\n+- CPU weight encoding by independent 64-row stripes, preserving the exact GPU\\n+  tile order while exposing enough parallel work.\\n+- Adaptive OpenMP team sizing and worker affinity, especially for an explicit\\n+  single KV worker.\\n+- Batched NumPy KV slot assignment instead of a per-block Python loop.\\n+- Weight encode defaults to CPU. GPU encode remains only behind the explicit\\n+  debug override `TIERED_SERVE_CD3_WEIGHT_CPU_ENCODE=0`.\\n+\\n+### 9.2 Standalone GPU decoder\\n+\\n+- Aligned two-`uint32` loading of each six-byte/48-bit code chunk. The final\\n+  thread reads bytes 1528..1535, so it never crosses a 1536-byte tile.\\n+- 128 physical threads per tile, each handling two logical 16-value chunks,\\n+  improving occupancy relative to the original 256-thread layout.\\n+- Two half-width live output accumulators followed by the same two 128-bit\\n+  stores, reducing register pressure. This improved aggregate layer-40 weight\\n+  decode by about 1.4%.\\n+- 2-D `(tile, block)` KV launch geometry.\\n+- Direct-to-final-cache indexed KV decode, eliminating the intermediate raw\\n+  BF16 copy.\\n+\\n+### 9.3 KV transport and orchestration\\n+\\n+- One aligned slot-major blob containing codes, sign/mantissa, bases, offsets,\\n+  and Packed20x3 entries, with explicit row strides in CPU/GPU kernels.\\n+- One `cudaMemcpyBatchAsync` submission for all selected K/V staging runs.\\n+- Selected-run staging for partial restores instead of copying every slot up\\n+  to the high-water mark.\\n+- Cached block/slot GPU metadata and one native paired K/V decode dispatch.\\n+- Active-set identity/content caching with generation-safe invalidation.\\n+- Dense steady-state plans that skip filtering, sorting, and contiguous-run\\n+  reconstruction.\\n+\\n+### 9.4 Fused Hopper kernel\\n+\\n+- Decode directly into WGMMA register A; no dense BF16 weight HBM write.\\n+- Expand each K tile's Packed20 exceptions into a shared exponent map before\\n+  WGMMA. This avoids P4Flat-style post-GEMM rank-1 correction for CD3's much\\n+  larger exception population.\\n+- Aligned two-`uint32` shared code extraction and register caching of each\\n+  48-bit code word rather than repeated byte loads.\\n+- One reusable exception-map stage for TileN<=64 and TileN=128.\\n+- A two-entry code ring for TileN=64 and a two-entry B ring for TileN=128,\\n+  reducing shared memory and raising GH200 residency to approximately five and\\n+  four CTAs respectively.\\n+- Native TileN=16 and TileN=32 paths to avoid padding small batches to 64.\\n+- N=48 specialization that reuses one decoded A fragment for standard WGMMA\\n+  N=32 plus N=16 instructions.\\n+- Shape/N-specific split-K: split-4 for all production projections through\\n+  N=64; at N=128, QKV/O/Down use split-4 while GateUp stays split-1.\\n+- Exact `Npad`: 16, 32, 48, then multiples of 64. This avoids P4Flat's\\n+  small-M N=128-to-192 widening behavior.\\n+- Projection-specific fused cutoff overrides and exact-length Packed20x3\\n+  staging views for calibration and variable exception streams.\\n+\\n+### 9.5 Routing and reproducibility\\n+\\n+- All four Llama-70B projection shapes are fused through N=128.\\n+- The standalone/raw prefill cutoff is 1856 and remains overrideable with\\n+  `TIERED_SERVE_PREFILL_ALT_MAX_CHUNK`.\\n+- Startup route reporting reflects projection-specific overrides.\\n+- The current 27-file implementation is stored as one checksum-verified,\\n+  idempotent HFS startup patch.\\n+\\n+## 10. Investigated but not retained\\n+\\n+- Reserving code 7 as a sentinel: invalid for current CD3 and materially worse\\n+  compression/decode behavior.\\n+- Removing the exception mask while keeping code 0: incorrect because normal\\n+  delta 0 and an exception would be indistinguishable.\\n+- Sparse warp-owner exception pre-application: about 2.3x slower.\\n+- Full `tCrA` ping-pong/double buffering: register count crossed the occupancy\\n+  cliff; rejected.\\n+- Exception-map-only overlap/epoch-map variants: no stable final win and not\\n+  present in the production kernel.\\n+- Pairwise standalone three-`uint32` loading: neutral or regressive; retained\\n+  loader is the aligned two-word loader per 48-bit chunk.\\n+- 64-thread and register-forced 10-CTA kernels: 7--12% slower.\\n+- Separate standalone exception kernel: 50.7% slower than integrated patching.\\n+- Direct-to-persistent Packed20 CPU packing: 3--6% slower at medium/large N.\\n+- Broad SVE2 rewrites: slower; only targeted predicate counting was retained.\\n+\\n+## 11. Hardware/model fit and retuning requirements\\n+\\n+| Area | Portability | What must be checked elsewhere |\\n+|:---|:---|:---|\\n+| Packed20x3 format, block-owner pass, min/max fast path | General CD3/CPU optimization | Verify exception rate and 64x64 divisibility for the new model |\\n+| NEON packing/extraction | ARM-specific | On Intel/AMD the scalar fallback works, but AVX2/AVX-512 should be implemented to retain encode speed |\\n+| SVE2 exception counting | ARM SVE2-specific | Confirm SVE2 availability/vector length; otherwise scalar fallback is used |\\n+| Worker affinity/adaptive OpenMP | Grace CPU/NUMA tuned | Retune worker count, CPU sets, NUMA placement, and OpenMP team size |\\n+| WGMMA register-A kernel | SM90/Hopper design | Rebuild and profile on H100, B200, or GB200; check instruction support, registers, shared memory, and spills |\\n+| 5/4 CTA residency and split-K table | GH200-specific tuning | Re-sweep active CTAs and split-K 1/2/4 for each GPU and exact `(M,K,N)` |\\n+| Projection shape table | Llama-3.1-70B TP=1-specific | Tensor parallelism or another model changes M/K and can invalidate every split/cutoff choice |\\n+| N=16/32/48/64/128 specializations | Workload-distribution fit | Keep if serving actually spends most decode steps in these N buckets |\\n+| Exception-map cost | LLM-weight-distribution fit | Measure exceptions for every layer; layer-0 QKV already demonstrates an outlier |\\n+| KV 2-D/direct-cache kernel | Tiered-serve `[16,8,128]` KV layout fit | Validate block size, head count/head dimension, row strides, and cache index mapping |\\n+| H2D savings | GH200 unified Grace/LPDDR/HBM fabric fit | Re-measure on PCIe, NVLink, B200, or GB200; byte savings remain, latency crossover changes |\\n+| Fused cutoff 128 | GH200 + Llama-70B + concurrent H2D fit | Re-sweep fused versus standalone under actual serving contention |\\n+| Prefill cutoff 1856 | Full `cao-cd3-fused` configuration fit | Re-measure standalone versus raw with the target model, residency map, chunking, and fabric |\\n+\\n+The general algorithmic changes are not overfit: fewer passes, fewer bytes,\\n+batched copies, cached plans, and avoiding an intermediate dense KV buffer are\\n+portable. The numeric policies are deliberately hardware/model fits. The\\n+highest-risk values to carry unchanged are split-K, CTA residency assumptions,\\n+the four Llama projection shapes, fused N=128, and prefill N=1856.\\n+\\n+## 12. Recommended port-validation sequence\\n+\\n+1. Measure compression ratio and exception histograms for all target-model\\n+   layers and real KV blocks.\\n+2. Measure CPU weight/KV encode with the target ISA. Add AVX2/AVX-512 on x86\\n+   before judging the ARM-vectorized implementation.\\n+3. Verify bit-exact resident weight and KV decode, then measure it separately\\n+   from H2D.\\n+4. Measure H2D-only with the target CPU/GPU fabric and actual residency split.\\n+5. Profile registers, shared memory, spills, active CTAs, barrier stalls, and\\n+   tensor-pipe utilization for N=16/32/48/64/128.\\n+6. Re-sweep split-K for every production projection shape and tensor-parallel\\n+   configuration.\\n+7. Recalibrate fused/standalone and standalone/raw boundaries under concurrent\\n+   serving H2D.\\n+8. Only then run full TTFT/TPOT comparisons against P4Flat. Do not infer full\\n+   serving speedup by summing isolated microbenchmarks.\\n+\\n+## 13. Result and source locations\\n+\\n+- Consolidated implementation manifest:\\n+  `/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/CD3_CURRENT_MANIFEST.md`\\n+- Consolidated startup patch:\\n+  `/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/cd3_current.patch`\\n+- Final fused comparison CSVs:\\n+  `/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/final-comparison/`\\n+- Standalone/raw prefill report:\\n+  `/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/PREFILL_ROUTE_REPORT.md`\\n+- Fused route/all-layer raw logs and CSVs:\\n+  `/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/`\\n*** End Patch\";\nconst result = await tools.apply_patch(patch);\ntext(result);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a05bee-b4d9-7d91-9c13-042c2f3dc999","create_time":1788249301.390745}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T07:56:17.444Z","ordinal":13977,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a05669-fc4c-7aa0-b9a1-034d833ed8ed","turn_id":"01a05bee-b4d9-7d91-9c13-042c2f3dc999","item":{"type":"FileChange","id":"exec-333350ad-53d4-4b2f-8d59-f1df98e2906d","changes":{"/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/CD3_OPTIMIZATION_AND_P4FLAT_COMPARISON.md":{"type":"add","content":"# CD3 optimization and P4Flat comparison\n\nDate: 2026-09-01 UTC\n\n## 1. Executive conclusion\n\nThe final CD3 implementation is not uniformly faster than P4Flat in every\nisolated operation. Its advantage comes from combining a denser lossless\nformat with substantially faster CPU encoding, less LPDDR-to-HBM traffic, and\na Hopper WGMMA fused path tuned for the decode batch sizes used by the serving\nworkload.\n\nOn the tested GH200 and Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct configuration:\n\n- Total compressed weight size is about 3.1% smaller: CD3 ratio is about\n  1.368x versus P4Flat 1.326x.\n- The LPDDR-resident portion transferred per layer in frozen-split mode is\n  376.586 MiB for CD3 versus 414.502 MiB for P4Flat, 9.15% fewer bytes.\n- CPU weight encoding of one layer's four fused projections is 14.9x to 28.1x\n  faster across the recorded runs. The latest median-of-five run was\n  128.830 ms versus 3620.297 ms.\n- Steady-state CPU KV encoding is 1.20x to 2.88x faster over N=8..2048.\n- Weight standalone H2D+decode is 4.36% faster overall. The H2D part is 10.89%\n  faster, while isolated resident decode is 1.45% slower.\n- In the latency-critical fused route, the sum of the four layer-40 projection\n  medians is 7.08% faster than P4Flat at N=64 and 14.62% faster at N=128 when\n  each codec uses its production route.\n- KV H2D-only and resident-decode-only are consistently faster in CD3, but the\n  complete production `decompress_batch` call is dispatch-dominated at small\n  N. Repeated fresh-process results range from 6--12% slower at N=8/64/256 to\n  statistical parity at N=1024. It is not valid to claim a robust KV\n  H2D+decode win at small N from the current measurements.\n\nThere is not yet a clean final-revision, apples-to-apples full serving\nTTFT/TPOT run of final CD3 against final P4Flat. The percentages above are\ncomponent and per-layer route measurements. Older 2026-08-27 serving results\npredate the final CD3 kernels and routing, so they are intentionally excluded\nfrom the headline comparison.\n\n## 2. Test platform and terminology\n\n- GPU/system: NVIDIA GH200, compute capability 9.0, Grace ARM CPU, CUDA 13.2.\n- Model weights: real `meta-llama/Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct`, TP=1, BF16.\n- Representative layer: transformer layer 40 unless otherwise stated.\n- Fused serving weights:\n  - QKV: 10240 x 8192, 160 MiB raw BF16.\n  - O: 8192 x 8192, 128 MiB.\n  - GateUp: 57344 x 8192, 896 MiB.\n  - Down: 8192 x 28672, 448 MiB.\n  - Total per layer: 1632 MiB (1.59375 GiB).\n- `N` is the flattened activation row/token count presented to a linear layer.\n- All retained codec paths are lossless; reported comparisons passed raw BF16\n  bit-exact checks.\n\nThe measurements use the following distinct definitions:\n\n- CPU encode: BF16 input is on the CPU. Time includes production codec CPU\n  work and synchronization, but never a CUDA encode kernel.\n- Resident decode: compressed data is already in GPU HBM. Only the GPU decode\n  kernel is timed.\n- H2D-only: only the LPDDR/pinned-host buffers required for the selected path\n  are copied into preallocated HBM staging. On GH200 this is Grace LPDDR to GPU\n  HBM over NVLink-C2C, not a conventional discrete-GPU PCIe transfer.\n- H2D+decode: the production transfer and decode operation is timed. It is an\n  independent measurement, not necessarily the arithmetic sum of separately\n  measured H2D and decode medians.\n- Fused: compressed weight reconstruction occurs in the register-A WGMMA\n  kernel and no dense BF16 weight is emitted to HBM.\n- Standalone: the full BF16 weight is reconstructed in HBM and then consumed\n  by a separate GEMM.\n- Raw: the full uncompressed BF16 weight is transferred and no codec decode\n  kernel runs.\n\n## 3. Format and memory footprint\n\nCD3 preserves the BF16 sign and mantissa byte verbatim and encodes the exponent\nas a 3-bit delta from one base exponent per 16 values. A 64x64 tile therefore\ncontains 1536 code bytes, 256 base bytes, and a sparse exception stream.\nExceptions use a 20-bit `(12-bit tile position, 8-bit exponent)` entry, packed\nthree entries into each 64-bit word (`Packed20x3`).\n\nCodes 0 through 7 are all normal CD3 deltas. An exception temporarily carries\ncode 0 and is distinguished by its Packed20 position/mask. Consequently code 7\ncannot be used as an exception sentinel without changing the format. The\nEscape7 experiment increased exceptions by 85.6%, slowed decode by 11.11%, and\nwould reduce the expected KV compression ratio to about 1.3347x, close to\nP4Flat's 1.3259x.\n\nFor representative layer-40 weights:\n\n| Metric | CD3 | P4Flat | CD3 change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Total compression ratio | 1.368x | 1.326x | 3.1% fewer compressed bytes |\n| Frozen-split LPDDR transfer/layer | 376.586 MiB | 414.502 MiB | 9.15% fewer bytes |\n\nBoth codecs retain sign/mantissa in HBM for frozen-split weights. CD3 also\nretains tile exception offsets in HBM. The table includes all transferred\nmetadata, so auxiliary structures are accounted for in the comparison.\n\nKV is different: offloaded KV does not retain the sign/mantissa half in HBM.\nThe selected K and V compressed slot blobs are transferred in full and decoded\ndirectly into the final GPU KV cache.\n\n## 4. CPU encoding\n\n### 4.1 Weight CPU encode\n\nInput was the four real layer-40 fused BF16 CPU tensors listed above. Each\ncodec ran in a fresh process on NUMA node 0, with one untimed warm-up and five\ntimed repetitions per projection; the table contains medians. No GPU encoder\nwas called.\n\n| Projection | CD3 (ms) | P4Flat (ms) | Speedup |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| QKV | 15.824 | 354.944 | 22.4x |\n| O | 12.710 | 285.589 | 22.5x |\n| GateUp | 79.161 | 1987.135 | 25.1x |\n| Down | 21.135 | 992.629 | 47.0x |\n| Sum of medians | 128.830 | 3620.297 | 28.1x |\n\nAn earlier recorded run was 130.7 ms versus 1951.9 ms (14.9x). CD3 is stable\naround 129--131 ms, while P4Flat CPU time is sensitive to host/OpenMP state.\nThe defensible conclusion is an order-of-magnitude win, observed between\n14.9x and 28.1x, rather than one universal multiplier.\n\nWeight encode is normally an offline/load-time step, so this gain mostly\nreduces initialization/preprocessing time and prevents codec preparation from\nstealing GPU SM cycles; it is not added to token latency.\n\n### 4.2 KV CPU encode\n\nInput was real cached K and V blocks from\n`/root/projects/ats-benchmarks/benchmarks/compress/kv_blocks_cache.pt`, each\nblock shaped `[16,8,128]` BF16. K+V is 64 KiB per block. The production\n`compress_batch_async_from_host` plus `wait_compress_async` path was measured\nwith one compression worker, `OMP=all`, NUMA node 0, one warm-up and 20 timed\niterations. GPU decode was used only after timing to verify bit exactness.\n\n| Blocks N | K+V input | CD3 median | P4Flat median | CD3 speedup |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 8 | 0.5 MiB | 0.17 ms | 0.49 ms | 2.88x |\n| 64 | 4 MiB | 0.28 ms | 0.55 ms | 1.96x |\n| 128 | 8 MiB | 0.40 ms | 0.60 ms | 1.50x |\n| 256 | 16 MiB | 0.49 ms | 0.68 ms | 1.39x |\n| 1024 | 64 MiB | 1.04 ms | 1.25 ms | 1.20x |\n| 2048 | 128 MiB | 1.99 ms | 2.48 ms | 1.25x |\n\n## 5. Weight standalone H2D/decode comparison\n\nThis used `benchmarks/weight_cpu_h2d_decode.py` with the real layer-40 fused\nweights, CPU encoding for both codecs, 10 warm-ups and 50 samples. Encode and\nsetup were outside CUDA timing. Sign/mantissa stayed in HBM; only the\nLPDDR-side exponent representation was transferred using the same\n`cudaMemcpyBatchAsync` helper for both codecs.\n\n| Sum across QKV/O/GateUp/Down | CD3 | P4Flat | CD3 change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| H2D-only | 1.2027 ms | 1.3497 ms | 10.89% faster |\n| Resident decode-only | 0.9211 ms | 0.9079 ms | 1.45% slower |\n| Serialized H2D+decode | 2.1286 ms | 2.2256 ms | 4.36% faster |\n\nThus the weight win is a transfer win, not a pure decoder win. CD3's 3-bit\ncodes reduce the LPDDR traffic enough to compensate for a slightly more\nexpensive decoder. Per-projection H2D+decode medians were also all lower for\nCD3: QKV 0.2183 vs 0.2338 ms, O 0.1808 vs 0.1904 ms, GateUp 1.1430 vs\n1.1920 ms, and Down 0.5864 vs 0.6095 ms.\n\n## 6. KV H2D/decode comparison\n\nThe input was the same real K/V block cache as the CPU encode test. Each codec\nand block count ran in a new process with CPU encoding, one worker, 20 warm-ups\nand 100 CUDA-event samples. Three complete rounds were run; the table reports\nthe median of the three per-process medians. No GPU encoder was called.\n\n| N | CD3 H2D | P4 H2D | CD3 resident decode | P4 resident decode | CD3 production total | P4 production total |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 8 | 0.0171 | 0.0289 | 0.0309 | 0.0458 | 0.0703 | 0.0629 |\n| 64 | 0.0260 | 0.0376 | 0.0287 | 0.0448 | 0.0686 | 0.0647 |\n| 256 | 0.0559 | 0.0666 | 0.0371 | 0.0466 | 0.1004 | 0.0915 |\n| 1024 | 0.1730 | 0.1849 | 0.0668 | 0.0736 | 0.2486 | 0.2497 |\n\nAll values are milliseconds. CD3 reduces H2D-only by 40.8%, 30.9%, 16.1%,\nand 6.4%, and resident decode by 32.5%, 35.9%, 20.4%, and 9.2% at the four\nsizes. However, the complete production call is 11.8%, 6.0%, and 9.7% slower\nat N=8/64/256 and only 0.4% faster at N=1024. At these sub-0.1-ms sizes,\nPython/native dispatch, plan lookup, stream/event handling, and K/V submission\noverhead dominate the kernels that were optimized.\n\nAn earlier single measurement recorded a more favorable CD3 production total\n(0.065/0.065/0.102/0.251 ms versus P4Flat 0.063/0.070/0.107/0.302 ms). The\nfresh three-round result above supersedes that single run for conclusions: the\nseparate H2D and decode improvements are robust, while small-N production\ntotal ordering is not.\n\n## 7. Fused decode+GEMM comparison\n\nInput weights were the four real layer-40 projections. Activations were seeded\nrandom BF16 `[N,K]` tensors. Sign/mantissa stayed resident in HBM and the\nLPDDR-side metadata was copied before the compute stream ran. L2 was flushed\nfor every sample; results are P50 after 10 warm-ups and 50 iterations. The\nnumbers below sum the four independently measured projection medians, so they\nare a per-layer component estimate, not an end-to-end TPOT result.\n\nCodec encode was setup only and excluded from timing. CD3 setup used its\ndefault CPU weight encoder; the P4Flat benchmark helper prepared its object\nwith the existing GPU encoder. This does not affect the timed H2D/kernel\ncomparison because both produce the production bit-exact format, but it must\nnot be read as an encode comparison.\n\n| N | P4Flat production route | CD3 production route | P4 total | CD3 total | CD3 gain |\n|---:|:---|:---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 32 | all fused | all fused | 2.2400 ms | 2.0096 ms | 10.28% |\n| 48 | all fused | all fused | 2.2539 ms | 2.0574 ms | 8.72% |\n| 64 | all fused | all fused | 2.2644 ms | 2.1041 ms | 7.08% |\n| 128 | QKV standalone, other three fused | all fused | 2.6661 ms | 2.2762 ms | 14.62% |\n\nAt N=128, forcing P4Flat QKV to remain fused gives 2.8080 ms versus CD3\n2.2756 ms, a 18.96% total reduction. The forced fused-kernel-only sums are\n1.5953 ms versus 1.1727 ms, a 26.49% reduction. This forced result is useful\nfor comparing kernels, but the 14.62% production-policy result is the fair\nserving route comparison.\n\nRelative to the early CD3 fused baseline discussed during development:\n\n- At N=64, CD3 kernel sum moved from 1.322 ms to 0.996 ms, about 24.7% lower.\n  It changed from 22.9% slower than P4Flat (1.076 ms) to 5.1% faster than the\n  current P4Flat kernel sum (1.050 ms).\n- At N=128, CD3 moved from 1.573 ms to 1.173 ms, about 25.4% lower. Its\n  forced-fused lead over P4Flat grew from about 4.6% to 26.5%.\n\n## 8. Production route policy\n\nThe final CD3 policy for the tested model/configuration is:\n\n- N <= 128: all QKV/O/GateUp/Down use fused decode+WGMMA.\n- Eager prefill N=129..1855: transfer compressed metadata, standalone-decode\n  the weight, then run GEMM.\n- Eager prefill N >= 1856: transfer raw BF16 and skip codec decode.\n\nCurrent P4Flat uses QKV fused only through N=64 and O/GateUp/Down through\nN=128. Its frozen-split standalone/raw prefill cutoff is 1330.\n\nThe CD3 standalone/raw cutoff was measured end-to-end with Llama-70B, not\nderived only from a bandwidth model. Five runs were made at each point and the\nfirst-use sample was discarded. At N=1824, standalone was 470.965 ms versus\n489.595 ms raw; at N=1856, raw was 459.620 ms versus 480.025 ms standalone.\nRaw remained ahead through N=3072. An unforced run confirmed the route switch\nexactly at 1856.\n\nThe 1856 versus 1330 cutoff should not be presented as a 39.5% direct\nperformance speedup: it says that CD3 remains worth decoding over a 526-token\nwider range under this residency/fabric/model combination.\n\nAn all-80-layer CD3 QKV sweep found fused faster on 79/80 layers and saved\n0.508/0.595/1.211 ms in aggregate at N=80/96/128 versus QKV standalone. Layer\n0 was the exception-heavy outlier. At full engine level, all-CD3-fused versus\nCD3-QKV-standalone TPOT P50 was 171.90/188.40/219.26 ms versus\n172.20/187.96/219.30 ms for batch 80/96/128: effectively tied because other\nserving work masks the per-layer difference. This is a CD3 route ablation, not\na CD3-versus-P4Flat end-to-end result.\n\n## 9. Retained optimization work\n\n### 9.1 CPU encoder\n\n- Block-owner one-pass encoding: one OpenMP owner completes a KV block in\n  tile/thread/item order, eliminating global prefix buffers, barriers, and the\n  second source-exponent read.\n- Min/max exception-free fast path: if 16 exponents fit in one 3-bit range,\n  candidate search and per-element exception work are skipped.\n- Direct local Packed20x3 construction with uninitialized temporary exception\n  scratch; only valid entries are compacted.\n- NEON extraction of exponent/sign-mantissa and hierarchical packing of 16\n  three-bit codes.\n- SVE2 predicate counting only on the uncommon exception path.\n- CPU weight encoding by independent 64-row stripes, preserving the exact GPU\n  tile order while exposing enough parallel work.\n- Adaptive OpenMP team sizing and worker affinity, especially for an explicit\n  single KV worker.\n- Batched NumPy KV slot assignment instead of a per-block Python loop.\n- Weight encode defaults to CPU. GPU encode remains only behind the explicit\n  debug override `TIERED_SERVE_CD3_WEIGHT_CPU_ENCODE=0`.\n\n### 9.2 Standalone GPU decoder\n\n- Aligned two-`uint32` loading of each six-byte/48-bit code chunk. The final\n  thread reads bytes 1528..1535, so it never crosses a 1536-byte tile.\n- 128 physical threads per tile, each handling two logical 16-value chunks,\n  improving occupancy relative to the original 256-thread layout.\n- Two half-width live output accumulators followed by the same two 128-bit\n  stores, reducing register pressure. This improved aggregate layer-40 weight\n  decode by about 1.4%.\n- 2-D `(tile, block)` KV launch geometry.\n- Direct-to-final-cache indexed KV decode, eliminating the intermediate raw\n  BF16 copy.\n\n### 9.3 KV transport and orchestration\n\n- One aligned slot-major blob containing codes, sign/mantissa, bases, offsets,\n  and Packed20x3 entries, with explicit row strides in CPU/GPU kernels.\n- One `cudaMemcpyBatchAsync` submission for all selected K/V staging runs.\n- Selected-run staging for partial restores instead of copying every slot up\n  to the high-water mark.\n- Cached block/slot GPU metadata and one native paired K/V decode dispatch.\n- Active-set identity/content caching with generation-safe invalidation.\n- Dense steady-state plans that skip filtering, sorting, and contiguous-run\n  reconstruction.\n\n### 9.4 Fused Hopper kernel\n\n- Decode directly into WGMMA register A; no dense BF16 weight HBM write.\n- Expand each K tile's Packed20 exceptions into a shared exponent map before\n  WGMMA. This avoids P4Flat-style post-GEMM rank-1 correction for CD3's much\n  larger exception population.\n- Aligned two-`uint32` shared code extraction and register caching of each\n  48-bit code word rather than repeated byte loads.\n- One reusable exception-map stage for TileN<=64 and TileN=128.\n- A two-entry code ring for TileN=64 and a two-entry B ring for TileN=128,\n  reducing shared memory and raising GH200 residency to approximately five and\n  four CTAs respectively.\n- Native TileN=16 and TileN=32 paths to avoid padding small batches to 64.\n- N=48 specialization that reuses one decoded A fragment for standard WGMMA\n  N=32 plus N=16 instructions.\n- Shape/N-specific split-K: split-4 for all production projections through\n  N=64; at N=128, QKV/O/Down use split-4 while GateUp stays split-1.\n- Exact `Npad`: 16, 32, 48, then multiples of 64. This avoids P4Flat's\n  small-M N=128-to-192 widening behavior.\n- Projection-specific fused cutoff overrides and exact-length Packed20x3\n  staging views for calibration and variable exception streams.\n\n### 9.5 Routing and reproducibility\n\n- All four Llama-70B projection shapes are fused through N=128.\n- The standalone/raw prefill cutoff is 1856 and remains overrideable with\n  `TIERED_SERVE_PREFILL_ALT_MAX_CHUNK`.\n- Startup route reporting reflects projection-specific overrides.\n- The current 27-file implementation is stored as one checksum-verified,\n  idempotent HFS startup patch.\n\n## 10. Investigated but not retained\n\n- Reserving code 7 as a sentinel: invalid for current CD3 and materially worse\n  compression/decode behavior.\n- Removing the exception mask while keeping code 0: incorrect because normal\n  delta 0 and an exception would be indistinguishable.\n- Sparse warp-owner exception pre-application: about 2.3x slower.\n- Full `tCrA` ping-pong/double buffering: register count crossed the occupancy\n  cliff; rejected.\n- Exception-map-only overlap/epoch-map variants: no stable final win and not\n  present in the production kernel.\n- Pairwise standalone three-`uint32` loading: neutral or regressive; retained\n  loader is the aligned two-word loader per 48-bit chunk.\n- 64-thread and register-forced 10-CTA kernels: 7--12% slower.\n- Separate standalone exception kernel: 50.7% slower than integrated patching.\n- Direct-to-persistent Packed20 CPU packing: 3--6% slower at medium/large N.\n- Broad SVE2 rewrites: slower; only targeted predicate counting was retained.\n\n## 11. Hardware/model fit and retuning requirements\n\n| Area | Portability | What must be checked elsewhere |\n|:---|:---|:---|\n| Packed20x3 format, block-owner pass, min/max fast path | General CD3/CPU optimization | Verify exception rate and 64x64 divisibility for the new model |\n| NEON packing/extraction | ARM-specific | On Intel/AMD the scalar fallback works, but AVX2/AVX-512 should be implemented to retain encode speed |\n| SVE2 exception counting | ARM SVE2-specific | Confirm SVE2 availability/vector length; otherwise scalar fallback is used |\n| Worker affinity/adaptive OpenMP | Grace CPU/NUMA tuned | Retune worker count, CPU sets, NUMA placement, and OpenMP team size |\n| WGMMA register-A kernel | SM90/Hopper design | Rebuild and profile on H100, B200, or GB200; check instruction support, registers, shared memory, and spills |\n| 5/4 CTA residency and split-K table | GH200-specific tuning | Re-sweep active CTAs and split-K 1/2/4 for each GPU and exact `(M,K,N)` |\n| Projection shape table | Llama-3.1-70B TP=1-specific | Tensor parallelism or another model changes M/K and can invalidate every split/cutoff choice |\n| N=16/32/48/64/128 specializations | Workload-distribution fit | Keep if serving actually spends most decode steps in these N buckets |\n| Exception-map cost | LLM-weight-distribution fit | Measure exceptions for every layer; layer-0 QKV already demonstrates an outlier |\n| KV 2-D/direct-cache kernel | Tiered-serve `[16,8,128]` KV layout fit | Validate block size, head count/head dimension, row strides, and cache index mapping |\n| H2D savings | GH200 unified Grace/LPDDR/HBM fabric fit | Re-measure on PCIe, NVLink, B200, or GB200; byte savings remain, latency crossover changes |\n| Fused cutoff 128 | GH200 + Llama-70B + concurrent H2D fit | Re-sweep fused versus standalone under actual serving contention |\n| Prefill cutoff 1856 | Full `cao-cd3-fused` configuration fit | Re-measure standalone versus raw with the target model, residency map, chunking, and fabric |\n\nThe general algorithmic changes are not overfit: fewer passes, fewer bytes,\nbatched copies, cached plans, and avoiding an intermediate dense KV buffer are\nportable. The numeric policies are deliberately hardware/model fits. The\nhighest-risk values to carry unchanged are split-K, CTA residency assumptions,\nthe four Llama projection shapes, fused N=128, and prefill N=1856.\n\n## 12. Recommended port-validation sequence\n\n1. Measure compression ratio and exception histograms for all target-model\n   layers and real KV blocks.\n2. Measure CPU weight/KV encode with the target ISA. Add AVX2/AVX-512 on x86\n   before judging the ARM-vectorized implementation.\n3. Verify bit-exact resident weight and KV decode, then measure it separately\n   from H2D.\n4. Measure H2D-only with the target CPU/GPU fabric and actual residency split.\n5. Profile registers, shared memory, spills, active CTAs, barrier stalls, and\n   tensor-pipe utilization for N=16/32/48/64/128.\n6. Re-sweep split-K for every production projection shape and tensor-parallel\n   configuration.\n7. Recalibrate fused/standalone and standalone/raw boundaries under concurrent\n   serving H2D.\n8. Only then run full TTFT/TPOT comparisons against P4Flat. Do not infer full\n   serving speedup by summing isolated microbenchmarks.\n\n## 13. Result and source locations\n\n- Consolidated implementation manifest:\n  `/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/CD3_CURRENT_MANIFEST.md`\n- Consolidated startup patch:\n  `/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/cd3_current.patch`\n- Final fused comparison CSVs:\n  `/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/final-comparison/`\n- Standalone/raw prefill report:\n  `/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/PREFILL_ROUTE_REPORT.md`\n- Fused route/all-layer raw logs and CSVs:\n  `/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/`\n"}},"status":"completed","stdout":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA /lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/CD3_OPTIMIZATION_AND_P4FLAT_COMPARISON.md\n","stderr":""},"started_at_ms":1788249377435,"completed_at_ms":1788249377444}}
